{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reference value is zero, since there is no sure outcome (i.e., no outcome with 100% probability) in either lottery. The differences between each outcome and the reference value (zero) are simply the outcomes themselves: for Lottery A, the differences are -6000 (with 0.1%) and 0 (with 99.9%); for Lottery B, -3000 (with 0.2%) and 0 (with 99.8%). The mean difference for each lottery is calculated as the probability-weighted average of these differences: for A, it is (-6000) * 0.001 = -6; for B, (-3000) * 0.002 = -6. Thus, both lotteries have the same mean difference of -6. The decision reason would be indifferent between the two lotteries since the mean differences are equal.",
