  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the mean of the differences between outcomes and a reference point (zero in this case), which corresponds to expected value when reference = 0. However, both lotteries have the same expected value (1500), so the reason would be indifferent. The individual, however, emphasizes the higher probability of receiving something over nothing and expresses aversion to the high chance of zero in B. This suggests a focus on the probability of non-zero outcomes and risk avoidance, not on computing mean differences from a reference point. While outcomes and probabilities are considered by both, the individual does not appear to compute or refer to deviations from a reference value. The justification is more aligned with minimizing the chance of disappointment (getting nothing) than with evaluating mean differences from a reference. Therefore, the specific cognitive mechanism of the decision reason—reference-dependent evaluation of outcome differences—is not evident in the report.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

